WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:06.820
The first film compared two circuits carrying the same flow.

2
00:00:08.140 --> 00:00:11.400
How big is that flow, and what did measuring it first prove?

3
00:00:12.533 --> 00:00:15.653
For centuries, the standard account came from Galen.

4
00:00:16.853 --> 00:00:19.473
Blood was made continually from food in the liver.

5
00:00:20.773 --> 00:00:22.453
The tissues it reached used it up.

6
00:00:23.383 --> 00:00:27.683
William Harvey, a London physician, tested this on the human arm.

7
00:00:28.903 --> 00:00:30.923
He tied a band round the upper arm.

8
00:00:32.043 --> 00:00:34.123
Tied tight, it stopped the pulse below it.

9
00:00:35.067 --> 00:00:39.867
Tied more loosely, it let the arteries drive blood in, but stopped its return.

10
00:00:41.227 --> 00:00:42.947
The veins below the band swelled.

11
00:00:44.227 --> 00:00:47.407
So blood enters a limb by arteries, and leaves by veins.

12
00:00:48.517 --> 00:00:50.897
Then Harvey asked a question of quantity.

13
00:00:52.097 --> 00:00:56.677
He made small guesses, on purpose, for how much blood leaves the heart at each beat.

14
00:00:57.767 --> 00:01:00.987
He multiplied by the number of beats in half an hour.

15
00:01:02.247 --> 00:01:07.147
Even that low product was more blood than the whole body contains, and far more than

16
00:01:07.147 --> 00:01:08.147
food could replace.

17
00:01:09.217 --> 00:01:10.637
Now test the argument.

18
00:01:12.257 --> 00:01:15.197
Suppose his guess per beat was still too big, by a full half.

19
00:01:16.497 --> 00:01:17.777
Does his conclusion fall?

20
00:01:24.200 --> 00:01:30.300
It stands. Even with half the scoop, half an hour still overflows the body many

21
00:01:30.300 --> 00:01:34.180
times. The heart moves far more than the body holds.

22
00:01:35.283 --> 00:01:37.123
So the same blood must go round.

23
00:01:38.383 --> 00:01:42.863
It leaves the heart by the arteries and comes back by the veins, again and again.

24
00:01:44.243 --> 00:01:45.243
Blood circulates.

25
00:01:46.133 --> 00:01:48.653
Harvey's product now has a modern name.

26
00:01:50.053 --> 00:01:54.773
Cardiac output is one ventricle's heart rate times its stroke volume, the blood it

27
00:01:54.773 --> 00:01:55.773
ejects per beat.

28
00:01:56.717 --> 00:01:58.797
So the heart has two dials on its flow.

29
00:02:00.097 --> 00:02:01.777
One dial sets the rate of beating.

30
00:02:03.137 --> 00:02:05.357
The other sets how much blood each beat ejects.

31
00:02:07.050 --> 00:02:10.810
In a healthy mammal, the rate dial is the sinoatrial (SA) node.

32
00:02:12.050 --> 00:02:15.030
It is a patch of pacemaker muscle in the right atrium.

33
00:02:16.450 --> 00:02:19.830
Sympathetic nerves turn it up, and the vagus nerve turns it down.

34
00:02:20.883 --> 00:02:24.163
The stroke volume dial has three inputs of its own.

35
00:02:25.323 --> 00:02:29.883
They are how much the ventricle fills, how strongly it contracts, and the pressure

36
00:02:29.883 --> 00:02:31.003
it must push against.

37
00:02:32.183 --> 00:02:37.323
Over half an hour, rate times stroke volume adds up to far more blood than you hold.

38
00:02:38.523 --> 00:02:40.243
Food could never replace it.

39
00:02:41.243 --> 00:02:44.183
So the same few litres go round, again and again.
